# Useful Apply for AI agents: plan

Status: approved direction, 2026-10-20. Wasim accepted every recommendation in section 6 (now locked) and asked for a better export design (section 4a). Next: critique by Fable 5.1, GPT-6.1 Sol (medium) and DeepSeek Flash, then build.

Out of scope here, planned later: "Sign in with ChatGPT" and using a user's ChatGPT or Claude subscription to pay for generation. The service layer below keeps model calls behind one interface so that can plug in later.

## 1. The answer: one remote MCP server, packaged three ways

Build **one remote MCP server** at `https://usefulapply.com/mcp`, protected by OAuth that runs on our existing Better Auth login. Ship it as:

| Package | Who installs it | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| **Claude connector** (Anthropic connector directory) | Claude.ai, Desktop, mobile, Cowork users | The MCP server URL plus a listing |
| **ChatGPT plugin** (OpenAI app directory) | ChatGPT users | The same MCP server plus OpenAI's manifest and domain check |
| **Claude Code plugin** (Anthropic plugin directory + our own marketplace repo) | Claude Code users | A small public GitHub repo: `.mcp.json` pointing at the same server, plus a skill that teaches the job-application workflow |

Cursor, Codex, VS Code and every other MCP client connect to the same URL with no extra work.

Why this shape:
- Both directories require the same things: a public HTTPS MCP server, Streamable HTTP, OAuth with PKCE, and annotated tools. One server serves both.
- A plugin alone can't reach Claude.ai or ChatGPT users, and an API key can't pass either directory review (both need OAuth).
- Everything runs inside the existing Worker: no new Worker, no Durable Objects. Cloudflare's stateless `createMcpHandler` (`agents` v0.20 / MCP SDK v2) runs in a Next.js route handler. Cloudflare's `McpAgent` is deprecated, so we don't use it.

## 2. What the deep dive found

**Ready to reuse**
- The chat agent already has a server-side tool registry (`src/lib/agents/chat-tools/`) with 20+ tools: applications, status, delete, CV outline and structure edits, feed settings, run feed, save and dismiss jobs, credits, documents. It's the closest thing we have to an MCP tool layer.
- Credit safety is solid: `reserveCredits` / `captureReservation` / `releaseReservation` with idempotency keys, plus partial refunds for failed documents.
- Generation, regeneration, follow-ups, CV parsing and the whole job feed are already server-side.

**Blockers to fix first**
1. **No way for an agent to sign in.** Auth is cookie-only (Better Auth with just `nextCookies()`). We need an OAuth authorization server. Better Auth 1.7 removed `oidcProvider`. The current path is `@better-auth/mcp` (built on `@better-auth/oauth-provider`) plus `jwt()` and `@better-auth/cimd`.
2. **The browser saves the finished kit, not the server.** `agent-v2` writes only `generated_artifacts`. The canonical `generated_cv`, `cover_letter` and `email` columns are written by the browser's 2-second debounced sync (`useSupabaseSync.ts`). A kit generated headlessly never lands there, so later CV rewrites fail with `NO_CV_DATA`. This is also a latent web bug: close the tab within 2 seconds of finishing and the kit only survives through the `open-session.ts` fallback.
3. **Key actions exist only as Next.js server actions,** which nothing outside the web app can call: create an application from a pasted JD, save a CV, read an application and its kit, set tracker status, delete, read and write the profile.
4. **Downloads run in the browser.** The CV PDF is html2canvas (DOM only). The cover letter PDF/DOCX (jsPDF, docx) is pure JS and can move to the server as is. CV DOCX doesn't exist today.
5. **Generation is long.** A kit takes about 40 seconds on Sonnet 5.5 (from the 2026-10-12 eval), and the only path today is an SSE stream inside one request.

**Directory rules that shape the design**
- Every tool needs a `title` and `readOnlyHint` / `destructiveHint` (ChatGPT also wants `openWorldHint`). Read and write must be separate tools. No generic "call any endpoint" tool. Names of 64 characters or fewer.
- **ChatGPT bans selling credits or subscriptions inside the plugin,** including upsell. Existing users can spend what they already have. So: no checkout or pricing tools, and an out-of-credits error says so plainly and links to an informational page, never to Stripe. Anthropic bars promotional content, so the same rule applies there.
- Both need a reviewer account with sample data and no MFA or email codes, plus privacy policy, terms and support URLs. ChatGPT also needs a verified OpenAI organisation and a domain challenge file.
- The current MCP spec (2026-07-28) is stateless, prefers Client ID Metadata Documents (CIMD) over dynamic client registration (DCR), and moved long-running "tasks" to an optional extension that Claude Code doesn't support yet.

## 3. Architecture

```
Claude / ChatGPT / Claude Code / Cursor
        │  Streamable HTTP + Bearer JWT
        ▼
usefulapply.com/mcp  (Next route, stateless createMcpHandler, wrapped in requireMcpAuth)
        │
        ├── /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource, oauth-authorization-server
        ├── /api/auth/oauth2/*  (Better Auth MCP/OAuth provider, CIMD + DCR)
        ├── /oauth/consent      (new page: "Claude wants to access your Useful Apply account")
        │
        ▼
src/lib/services/*  (new: one server function per user capability, shared by MCP and web)
        │
        ├── D1 repos, credits, R2, Workers AI, Anthropic
        └── Cloudflare Queue consumer: runs kit generation off the request and persists it
```

Design points:
- **Auth:** access tokens are short-lived JWTs verified against our JWKS. Two scopes: `read` and `write` (write covers spending credits). Advertise CIMD and keep DCR on, since some clients still need it. Allowlist the Claude, Claude Code (loopback) and ChatGPT callbacks.
- **Connected apps:** a new Settings pane lists authorised agents with a Revoke button.
- **Same rules as every API route:** `userId` only from the verified token, a per-user rate-limit bucket for MCP, input length validation, generic error messages. Tool errors are specific and actionable ("No CV on file. Call save_cv first."), never stack traces.
- **Service layer:** extract the logic now locked in server actions and route handlers into `src/lib/services/` (applications, kit, cv, tracker, feed, exports). MCP tools call services. Web routes keep working; any of them we touch call the same services.
- **Kit persistence moves to the server:** a `persistKit(sessionId, results)` service writes the canonical columns when generation finishes. MCP needs this. I recommend switching the web flow to it too (decision 4).
- **Long generation:** `generate_application_kit` queues the job on a Cloudflare Queue, using the existing `application_sessions.generation_status` column. The tool waits up to about 45 seconds. If the kit is done it returns it, otherwise it returns "still generating, call `get_application`". This survives client timeouts and disconnects and works on every client, with or without the Tasks extension.
- **Files:** exports go to R2 and come back as an MCP `resource_link` with a short-lived signed download URL (the agent's client has no cookie), plus an "Open in Useful Apply" link to the application. Full design in section 4a.
- **Analytics:** a PostHog `mcp_tool_called` event with tool, client name, duration and outcome. No conversation or document content, which the directory rules forbid.
- **Anti-hallucination is unchanged:** generation always reads the CV from D1. The agent supplies the job description, never facts about the applicant. The only way to change those facts is `save_cv`, which is the user's own CV.

## 4. Tool catalog (v1, 24 tools)

Credit costs come from `src/lib/credit-costs.ts`. Every credit-spending tool states its cost in its description and returns `creditsUsed` and the new balance.

**Read** (`readOnlyHint: true`)

| Tool | Does |
|---|---|
| `get_account` | Plan, credit balance, next reset, whether a primary and master CV are on file, profile basics |
| `list_applications` | Tracker list with status filter and pagination |
| `get_application` | One application: job, fit result, status, dates, generation status, kit content (`format: concise \| full`) |
| `get_cv` | Primary or master CV, or the tailored CV for an application, as structured data |
| `list_cv_templates` | The 6 templates with market (US / EU) and photo support |
| `get_job_feed` | Inbox or saved jobs, paginated |
| `get_feed_settings` | Current feed preferences |

**Write: spends credits** (`readOnlyHint: false`, `destructiveHint: false`)

| Tool | Credits | Does |
|---|---|---|
| `analyze_job_fit` | 0.5 | Fit analysis from a job description. Creates a tracker entry |
| `generate_application_kit` | 1.5 after a fit check, else 2.0 | CV, cover letter, application email and follow-up, queued with a 45s wait |
| `regenerate_document` | 0.5 | Rewrite the cover letter, email or follow-up, with an optional instruction |
| `rewrite_cv_section` | 0.5 (free if refused) | Rewrite one CV section with an instruction |
| `apply_to_feed_job` | 0.5 or 2.0 | Turn a feed job into an application and run fit or the full kit |

**Write: free**

| Tool | Hint | Does |
|---|---|---|
| `save_cv` | destructive | Upload a CV (text, or a PDF/DOCX as base64), parse it, save it as primary or master |
| `edit_cv_structure` | not destructive | Reorder sections, rename headings (reuses the chat tool) |
| `delete_cv_item` | destructive | Remove an item from the tailored CV |
| `set_cv_template` | not destructive | Choose the template for an application |
| `update_application_status` | not destructive | Status plus interview and follow-up dates (these drive the reminder emails) |
| `delete_application` | destructive | Remove an application |
| `export_documents` | not destructive | CV PDF, cover letter PDF/DOCX, emails as text. Returns download links |
| `run_job_feed` | not destructive | Run today's feed (one run per day, consent required) |
| `update_feed_settings` | not destructive | Roles, countries, remote preference, salary floor |
| `give_job_feedback` | not destructive | Save, unsave, dismiss, rate a feed job |
| `update_profile` | not destructive | Contact details used in the CV header |
| `draft_screening_answers` | not destructive | Plus/Max screening-answers mode, as in chat |

**Left out on purpose:** checkout, plans and credit packs (both directories' commerce rules), account deletion, the chat endpoint itself (the agent is the chat), and admin.

## 4a. Exports: one renderer for people and agents

**Today:**
- The CV PDF is built in the browser with html2canvas. It's a picture of the page sliced into pages, so the text can't be selected and ATS parsers read it badly.
- The cover letter PDF is plain Helvetica drawn by jsPDF. Most letters are body-only (the writer agent returns one block of text), so there's no letterhead, no date and no matching design.

**New design: a print route rendered by Cloudflare Browser Run**
1. **`/print/cv/[sessionId]` and `/print/cover-letter/[sessionId]`.** These are server-rendered Next pages that use the same `CVTemplateRenderer` and the same CSS bundle as the on-screen canvas. The six templates have no browser-only hooks, so they render on the server as they are. Access is through a single-use render token that expires after 60 seconds, so the page is never public.
2. **`env.BROWSER.quickAction("pdf", { url })`** turns the page into a PDF with real, selectable text. Paper size follows the template (A4 for the German templates, US Letter for the rest), and fonts are embedded. Print CSS adds clean page breaks: no entry is split across pages, and headings stay with their content.
3. **The cover letter gets a real letter layout matched to the chosen CV template.** That means a letterhead with name and contact details from the CV, the date, company and role, then the body and signature, set in the template's fonts and accent colour. The PDF uses the same print route. The DOCX is built server-side with the existing `docx` library and has the same letterhead.
4. **Emails** export as `.txt` (subject plus body), and are also returned inline as text.
5. **People get the same files.** The web "Download PDF" buttons call the same server export. If Browser Run fails, the web app falls back to the current in-browser export, with a visible notice and a PostHog event, so a download never dead-ends. The MCP tool returns an error instead of a degraded file.
6. **`export_documents` takes `templateId`** (any of the 6) and `documents: cv_pdf | cover_letter_pdf | cover_letter_docx | emails_txt`. Files are cached in R2 by a hash of (content, template), so asking for the same export again costs nothing.

**Cost:**
- Browser Run on Workers Paid includes 10 browser-hours a month, then $0.09 an hour, and 10 concurrent browsers averaged monthly ($2 per extra). At about 3 seconds per PDF, that's roughly 12,000 PDFs a month free, then about $0.0001 per PDF.
- Cloudflare Queues on Workers Paid: about 1M operations a month included, then about $0.40 per million. Our volume stays inside the included amount. Confirm on the pricing page during PR 2.
- Expected monthly bill at today's volume: $0.

**Verification:**
- An E2E spec renders all 6 templates for two fixtures: a one-page US CV, and a two-page German CV with photo and long entries.
- For each one it checks that the PDF has selectable text (pdf.js extracts the name and every section heading), has the right page size and never splits an entry across a page.
- It saves page images next to screenshots of the on-screen canvas.
- Visual parity is judged as the global rules require: a clean Opus judge, plus your own verdict on the same pairs.

**Claude Code plugin skill:** one skill, `apply-to-job`, that teaches the flow: check the account, make sure a CV is on file, fit check, confirm the credit spend with the user, generate, review, export, set the tracker status. No other skills.

## 5. Delivery: 5 PRs

| PR | Contents | Review row | Depends on |
|---|---|---|---|
| **1. OAuth + MCP skeleton** | Better Auth MCP/OAuth provider, JWT, CIMD, D1 migration for the OAuth tables, consent page, well-known routes, Connected apps pane, `/mcp` with `get_account` only | Sensitive (Sonnet + Codex + Opus, high) | none |
| **2. Service layer + server-side kit persistence** | `src/lib/services/*`, `persistKit`, Queue-based generation runner, server-side CV save | Sensitive (touches credits and generation) | none, runs in parallel with PR 1 |
| **3. Tool catalog** | The 24 tools, annotations, rate limits, PostHog event | Feature (Sonnet + Codex, high) | 1 and 2 |
| **4. Exports** | Print routes, Browser Run binding, template-matched cover letter (PDF and DOCX), web download switched to the server export, R2 cache and signed links (section 4a) | Feature, plus a design judge for parity | 2 (the service that reads a kit) |
| **5. Distribution** | Public plugin repo with marketplace.json and skill, OpenAI manifest and domain challenge, `/integrations` docs page, privacy policy and terms update, seeded reviewer account | Small (Sonnet) plus copy polish | 3 |

PRs 1, 2 and 4 can be built in parallel by separate workers. Production deploys after each merge with `npm run deploy:cf`, and `/mcp` stays unlisted until PR 5.

**Verification (E2E, repeatable):** a checked-in script against `npm run preview` that:
- runs the full OAuth flow headless (Playwright logs in and approves consent);
- then, as an MCP client, uploads a two-page German CV, runs a fit check on a senior JD, generates the kit, deletes a CV item, rewrites a section, regenerates the cover letter with an instruction, exports the CV PDF and the cover letter DOCX, and moves the application to `interview` with a date;
- then opens `/dashboard` in Playwright and screenshots the same kit and tracker state.

Artifacts go to `verify/<branch>/`: the HTML report, the tool-call transcript and the exported files. After that I run live checks on Claude.ai (custom connector), Claude Code, MCP Inspector and ChatGPT developer mode.

**Submission:** you do this part, because it needs your accounts. Anthropic: claude.ai/directory/manage for the connector and the plugin. OpenAI: identity verification, then the app directory form. I'll prepare every field, the example prompts, the screenshots and the reviewer credentials file.

## 6. Decisions (all accepted by Wasim on 2026-10-20)

1. **CV and cover letter PDF on the server:** Browser Run print routes, used by people and agents alike (section 4a). **Accepted.** The web download switches too, with the in-browser export kept as a fallback.
2. **Cloudflare Queues for generation.** Included in Workers Paid, with roughly zero extra cost at our volume. The alternative is synchronous 40-second tool calls that some clients may time out on. **I recommend Queues.**
3. **Free tier over MCP.** Should free users (4 credits a month) get the full tool set, with the same gates as the web app? **I recommend yes:** same rules everywhere, and no upsell inside the plugin.
4. **Server-side kit persistence for the web app too.** Fixes the close-the-tab gap and keeps one write path. It changes the web generation flow, so it needs a careful E2E check. **I recommend yes,** in PR 2.
5. **Public GitHub repo** for the Claude Code plugin (for example `wasimjalali/usefulapply-plugin`). The Anthropic plugin directory requires public. OK to create?
6. **Legacy MCP clients.** Accept older stateless clients as well as the 2026-07-28 spec (**recommended**, since client rollout is uneven), or accept the new spec only.

## 7. Risks

- **ChatGPT commerce review.** The product is credit-metered. The rules allow existing users to use what they have, but a reviewer could still push back. Mitigation: no prices, plans or upgrade prompts anywhere in tool output.
- **Better Auth MCP packages are new.** I'll verify `@better-auth/mcp`, `@better-auth/cimd` and `@better-auth/oauth-provider` against the 1.7 docs and on D1 (no interactive transactions) before PR 1 goes further than a spike.
- **Unverified client behaviour:** exact ChatGPT redirect URIs (copied from its management page at setup), whether clients render PDF resources inline (the signed link is the fallback), and each client's tool-call timeout (the Queue design covers it).
- **Rate limits:** an agent can loop. Per-user MCP buckets plus the existing credit reservations cap the damage.
# AGENTS.md: Useful Apply Behavioral Instructions

> For historical context, architecture docs, feature narratives, and the changelog, check git history.

---

## Useful Apply Agents (use proactively, do not ask permission)

Specialist agents are the **preferred** way to handle bug investigations, external research, code review, UI verification, and agent/prompt work. They protect the main context window and often find issues faster than direct exploration. Spawn them in parallel when the work is independent.

### Project-scope agents (this repo)
- **`debugger`** (global agent; read `.claude/rules/debugging-patterns.md` first) — Whenever a runtime bug, regression, stack trace, or "X is broken but I don't know why" appears. Also for intermittent SSE/streaming failures, wrong state on screen, credit/billing anomalies, and any failed request without an obvious cause. Read-only by default; proposes patches with file:line, refuses to paper over symptoms.
- **`agent-expert`** — When editing anything in `src/lib/agents/`, `src/lib/agents/prompts/`, `src/lib/models/`, `src/app/api/agent-v2/`, or the `useAgentV2` / `useAgentWorkflow` hooks. Also for anti-hallucination rule changes, SSE event semantics, context-builder work, CV pipeline, fit-assessment prompts. Powered by Opus.
- **`ui-tester`** — After any UI change to `/dashboard`, `/applications`, `/documents`, `/` (landing), or shared components (ChatPanel, CVPanel, CoverLetterPanel, EmailPanel, FeedConversation, FeedCanvas). Uses Playwright MCP. Run before calling a UI task done.
- **`karko-architect`** — For cross-cutting design decisions, refactors, and "where should this state live" questions. Use for anything that spans 3+ files or has non-obvious tradeoffs. Returns a plan with ordered steps; does not modify code itself. Powered by Opus.
- **`d1-dba`** — When writing or reviewing D1 migrations in `migrations/d1/`, touching schema, or when a query looks suspicious. Will not run migrations itself. (Legacy `supabase/migrations/` is frozen until decommission.)
- **`integration-tester`** — Before every commit touching components, hooks, API routes, or job-feed files. Runs tsc + lint + narrow spot-checks. Cheap and fast — uses Sonnet.
- **`job-feed-specialist`** — When editing anything under `src/lib/job-feed/**`, `src/app/api/job-feed/**`, or `src/components/job-feed/**`. Owns ingestion, prefilter, LLM ranker, batch reconcile, and settings flows.
- **`karko-ui-designer`** — For UI/UX audits, redesigns, and visual polish. Carries brand tokens (navy/cream/gold), typography (Georgia+Arial), surface roles, radius/shadow scales, and anti-AI-slop rules in its system prompt. Can audit AND implement. Does NOT touch backend, API routes, data fetching, or Zustand stores.

### Opus escalation (automatic — do not wait to be asked)
For tasks requiring deep reasoning or premium creative output, draft first as Sonnet then escalate to Opus via Agent tool (model: "opus") to critique and polish. Pattern: Sonnet drafts → Opus reviews and produces final → present Opus's version to user.

Escalate automatically for:
- **Website / marketing copywriting** — homepage, landing pages, CTAs, onboarding copy, pricing copy
- **Video scripts** — demo scripts, walkthrough scripts, any voiceover narrative
- **Debugging non-trivial bugs** — use the `debugger` agent (already Opus). Don't try to root-cause stack traces or multi-file regressions in the main thread.
- **Anti-hallucination / prompt changes** — use the `agent-expert` agent (already Opus) for anything in `src/lib/agents/**`.
- **High-stakes UI/UX decisions** — brand-level design critique after `karko-ui-designer` runs
- **Architecture decisions** — anything spanning 3+ files (use `karko-architect` which already uses Opus)

### When to parallelize
If two agent tasks are independent (different files, different questions), spawn them in a **single message with multiple Agent tool calls** — they run concurrently.

---

## Working Rules

### Product Priorities (read before any feature work)
- **Quality > speed > cost.** Useful Apply is a pre-revenue startup; perceived quality is the moat. Do not introduce soft-constraint heuristic rejections (seniority regex, location substring matching, skill keyword scoring, work-mode keyword inference) when a language model can read the description and make the same call — LLM cost is negligible at our scale. Use deterministic prefilters ONLY for hard 99%+ reliable constraints (explicit employment type, explicit salary floor, explicit sponsorship language). Prefer prompt caching + Batch APIs to reduce cost over degrading the feature.
- **Desktop + tablet (≥768px) is the target. Mobile is NOT a priority.** Useful Apply is a desktop-class productivity tool (CV canvas, side-by-side chat + preview, multi-panel application kit). When making UI changes, verify desktop first. Mobile may look rough or stack layouts — don't chase mobile-specific bugs unless they affect desktop too. Complex flows (CV edit, chat, generation) will eventually be gated to a "best on desktop" prompt on mobile; don't over-engineer responsive variants for them.

### Autonomy & Speed
- Work autonomously — do NOT ask permission for bug fixes, UI changes, refactors, or small features
- Read only the specific files you need, then act — use Grep/Glob/Read directly, not subagents, for known targets
- Confirm before running a migration against `--remote` (production D1)
- Auth (Better Auth), Stripe and credits, and D1 schema changes are the Sensitive review row (Sonnet + Codex + Opus, high).

### After Every Code Change
- `npx tsc --noEmit` must return zero errors (TypeScript errors break the build)
- `npm run lint` when modifying components, hooks, or API routes
- Do NOT run `npm run build` unless asked

### Git Workflow
Follow the global change loop: a branch per change, in its own worktree. Branch names: `feature/...` / `fix/...`.

After the PR is open:
1. Review per the global Reviews rule (Sonnet, plus Codex for features, plus Opus for sensitive; at most 3 rounds).
2. Wait for GitHub CI checks to go green. Do not merge on red or pending checks.
3. Merge the PR into `main`, then deploy with `npm run deploy:cf`. Nothing deploys on merge, so production only moves when that command runs.

### Deployment
- Cloudflare Workers via `@opennextjs/cloudflare`. Production ships by running `npm run deploy:cf` from `main`. There is no auto-deploy and no GitHub Actions workflow, so a merge alone changes nothing on usefulapply.com.

### Security Rules
- Never read, log or modify `.dev.vars` (local secrets) or `.dev.vars.*` other than `.dev.vars.example`.
- Watch for: SQL injection, XSS, unprotected API routes, Stripe webhook bypass, secrets leaking into client-side code

### Database / D1
- **Never modify production DB schema directly** — always write a SQL migration in `migrations/d1/`, applied with `npx wrangler d1 migrations apply usefulapply-db`
- Migration filenames: descriptive snake_case (e.g., `fix_starter_credits_10.sql`)
- Before writing a migration, check existing migrations to avoid conflicts

### Token Efficiency & Subagent Rules

**Subagent model selection (mandatory):**
| Task Type | Model | When |
|-----------|-------|------|
| Explore/search | `sonnet` (agent: `sonnet-worker`) | Simple searches and quick lookups |
| Code analysis, multi-file investigation, planning | `sonnet` | Default for most dev subagents |
| Copywriting, video scripts, marketing copy | `opus` | Always — Sonnet drafts first, Opus polishes |
| Debugging with stack trace / unexpected behavior | `opus` | Via `debugger` agent |
| Architecture decisions, 3+ file refactors | `opus` | Via `karko-architect` |
| UI/UX critique, brand-level design decisions | `opus` | Via `karko-ui-designer` for high-stakes calls |

Always pass the `model` parameter when spawning an Agent. Never let subagents default to the parent model for expensive tasks.

**Web search limits:**
- Maximum **3 WebFetch calls** per subagent task unless explicitly told to do more
- Maximum **5 WebFetch calls** across all subagents for a single user request
- Always prefer documentation sites (docs.anthropic.com, docs.vercel.com) over blog posts or articles
- Do NOT search multiple websites for the same answer — if the first source answers it, stop

---

## Architecture Overview

Useful Apply is a Next.js 16 app generating job application materials (CV, Cover Letter, Application Email, Follow-Up Email) via a unified AI agent. Model split: Claude Sonnet 5.5 on the Anthropic API (`AI_PROVIDER=anthropic`) handles kit writing, CV parsing, context extraction and fit analysis, with Claude Sonnet 4.6 on Amazon Bedrock kept as the fallback (`AI_PROVIDER=bedrock`); `@cf/openai/gpt-oss-120b` on Workers AI (`src/lib/models/workers-ai.ts`) handles chat, tracker chat, career-profile synthesis, search strategy and job-feed ranking.

